---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import PlanningCTA from '../../components/PlanningCTA.astro';
import { groupTravelPages } from '../../data/landing';
import { images } from '../../data/assets';
const breadcrumbs=[{label:'Group Travel',href:'/group-travel/'}];
---
<BaseLayout title="Private Group Travel for Clubs, Communities & Teams | Talon Expeditions" description="Custom private group travel for hiking clubs, dive communities, wellness groups, businesses, social groups, and organizers, with worldwide planning and traveler support." image={images.group} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <section class="hub-hero" style={`background-image:url('${images.group}')`}><div class="shell"><Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs}/><p class="eyebrow">Group travel, built around the people</p><h1 class="section-title">Bring the community. We will help turn the shared idea into a trip.</h1><p class="lead">Talon Expeditions plans private and organizer-led group travel for clubs, communities, businesses, studios, teams, and affinity groups. The itinerary is shaped around who is actually traveling, why they want to go, and what the organizer needs to make the trip workable.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell definition-grid"><div class="definition-sticky"><p class="eyebrow">What group-first means</p><h2>The group is not an afterthought added to a standard package.</h2></div><div class="definition-body"><div class="answer-panel"><h2>What does Talon Expeditions do for group organizers?</h2><p>We help organizers define the trip, compare destinations and formats, build the travel structure, coordinate suppliers and logistics, communicate the practical requirements, prepare travelers, and keep the planning process moving toward a viable departure.</p></div><p>Private group travel can be simple or operationally complex. A group may all live in one city and want a long weekend. Another may have members arriving from several states, different rooming needs, technical activity requirements, and a full week of coordinated travel. The right support model depends on those realities.</p><p>We do not assume every group should look like a traditional tour group. Some want a guide or host throughout. Others want independent time with organized lodging, transport, and selected activities. Some are learning a skill. Others want a social or cultural journey where the group itself is the reason to travel. The planning should reflect that difference.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section soft-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Who we work with</p><h2 class="section-title">Different communities create different travel problems.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The pages below explain how trip design changes for specific organizer types, activity communities, and group structures.</p></div><div class="category-card-grid">{groupTravelPages.map((page)=><a class="category-card" href={`/group-travel/${page.slug}/`}><div class="category-card-image" style={`background-image:url('${page.hero}')`}></div><div class="category-card-body"><h3>{page.title}</h3><p>{page.description}</p><span>Explore this group type →</span></div></a>)}</div></div></section>
  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">The organizer side of the trip</p><h2 class="section-title">A good itinerary is only useful if people can understand, join, and prepare for it.</h2></div><p class="section-copy" style="color:#aaa">Group travel adds a second customer: the person responsible for making the trip happen. We plan with that role in mind.</p></div><div class="why-grid" style="border-color:#302a26"><div class="why-item"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Viability</h3><p style="color:#aaa">Test group size, timing, cost, destination, and activity assumptions before the trip is overcommitted.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">02</span><h3>Clarity</h3><p style="color:#aaa">Explain inclusions, pace, rooming, requirements, deadlines, and what participants need to decide.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">03</span><h3>Readiness</h3><p style="color:#aaa">Give travelers enough time to resolve documents, fitness, equipment, insurance, and activity prerequisites.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">04</span><h3>Coordination</h3><p style="color:#aaa">Connect supplier information, arrivals, accommodation, transport, activities, and group communication.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell process-donor"><div class="process-visual" style={`background-image:url('${images.planning}')`}><div class="process-callout"><h3>Start before the plan is finished.</h3><p>Early decisions are easier to change than confirmed supplier commitments.</p></div></div><div><p class="eyebrow">How it works</p><h2 class="section-title">A planning sequence designed for groups.</h2><p class="section-copy">We begin with the people and purpose, then move into the destination and logistics. That order usually produces a more realistic trip and clearer information for participants.</p><div class="process-list"><div class="process-item"><b>Define the group</b><p>Who is likely to travel, what connects them, and what constraints matter?</p></div><div class="process-item"><b>Shape the trip</b><p>Activity, destination, pace, comfort, support, timing, and approximate budget.</p></div><div class="process-item"><b>Build the travel system</b><p>Accommodation, transport, operators, activities, rooming, and dependencies.</p></div><div class="process-item"><b>Prepare the travelers</b><p>Documents, readiness, payment milestones, final information, and departure support.</p></div></div><a class="button" href="/how-it-works/">See the Full Planning Process</a></div></div></section>
  <PlanningCTA title="Your group does not need to fit an existing departure." copy="Start with the people, the purpose, or the kind of trip you want to create. We can help determine what is realistic from there." />
</BaseLayout>
